--- b6 medium.en: Next, is the math of the gain, and the IRS formula is simple. Take the selling price and subtract your selling costs, like the agent's commission, advertising, and legal fees. That gives you the amount realized. Then subtract your adjusted basis, which is what you invested in the home. What is left is your gain. If it is negative, you have a loss, and the IRS says you cannot deduct a loss on your home.
--- b6 large-v3: Next is the math of the gain, and the IRS formula is simple. Take the selling price and subtract your selling costs, like the agent's commission, advertising, and legal fees. That gives you the amount realized. then subtract your adjusted basis which is what you invested in the home what is left is your gain if it is negative you have a loss and the IRS says you cannot deduct a loss on your home
--- b7 medium.en: Your basis is more than the price you paid because it includes closing costs like legal fees, recording fees, transfer taxes and owner's title insurance, plus improvements, a new roof, a kitchen remodel, an addition, a new heating system. It does not include repairs like painting or fixing a leak or loan fees and points. Keep your receipts. The IRS says to keep records until three years after the due date of the return for the year you sell.
